# Skelly_Panels.ssv – Under-The-Hood panel routing table
#
# This file tells GUI_Skelly what internal plots exist and how to draw them.
# Each NON-comment, NON-blank line defines one plot that appears in the
# "Under The Hood" dropdown.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BASIC FORMAT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lines are semicolon-separated:
#
#   panel_id ; label ; kind ; files
#
# - panel_id : short internal name, no spaces (e.g. io_aff, devmap, trace_metrics)
# - label    : human-readable text shown in the GUI dropdown
# - kind     : tells the GUI how to interpret the file(s)
# - files    : one or more file paths, comma-separated if multiple
#
# All whitespace around fields is ignored. Lines beginning with "#" or "//"
# are treated as comments and ignored.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPPORTED "kind" VALUES AND EXPECTED FILE FORMATS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1) kind = io_log
#    ----------------
#    Use this for I/O logs where the first column is time/tick and the rest are
#    channel values. The GUI will plot one line per channel against tick.
#
#    Expected file format:
#
#       Tick  v0    v1    v2   ...
#       0     0.1   0.3   0.0
#       1     0.2   0.4   0.1
#       ...
#
#    - files: exactly ONE filename
#    - x-axis: Tick (first column)
#    - y-series: each remaining column (v0, v1, v2, ...)
#
#    Example:
#       io_aff ; Afferent I/O Log ; io_log ; System_State_Files/afferent_log.ssv
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 2) kind = matrix_rows
#    --------------------
#    Use this for 2D data that used to be plotted as heatmaps. Each row of the
#    matrix becomes a separate line series; the x-axis is the column index.
#
#    Expected file format:
#
#       index  v0    v1    v2   ...
#       0      0.1   0.2   0.3
#       1      0.4   0.5   0.6
#       2      0.7   0.8   0.9
#       ...
#
#    - The first column ("index") is ignored by the GUI.
#    - The remaining values in each row become a series:
#        row 0 -> series 0, row 1 -> series 1, etc.
#    - x-axis: 0, 1, 2, ... (column index)
#
#    Example:
#       devmap     ; Deviation Map    ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/deviation_mapping.ssv
#       projection ; Projection Map   ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/projection.ssv
#       scores     ; Output Scores    ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/output_scores.ssv
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 3) kind = xy
#    ----------
#    Use this for a single time-series where each row is (x, y).
#
#    Expected file format:
#
#       x    y
#       0    15.0
#       10   15.5
#       20   16.2
#       ...
#
#    - files: exactly ONE filename
#    - x-axis: first column
#    - y-series: second column (one line)
#    - series label in legend: basename of the file (e.g. temperature.ssv)
#
#    Example:
#       temp ; Temperature over time ; xy ; System_State_Files/temperature.ssv
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 4) kind = xy_multi
#    -----------------
#    Use this when you have several SSV files that share the same x-values
#    (e.g. tick) and you want them all plotted together as separate lines.
#
#    Expected file format for EACH file:
#
#       x    y
#       0    0.1
#       10   0.2
#       20   0.15
#       ...
#
#    - files: one or more filenames, comma-separated
#      (e.g. fileA.ssv,fileB.ssv,fileC.ssv)
#    - The GUI:
#        * uses the first non-empty file’s x-column as the master x-axis
#        * adds one series per file (y-column)
#        * ignores any file whose length doesn’t match the master x length
#        * labels each series with the basename of the file
#
#    Example:
#       trace_metrics ; Trace / Node Metrics ; xy_multi ;
#           System_State_Files/trace_valid.ssv,
#           System_State_Files/trace_nearly_valid.ssv,
#           System_State_Files/trace_total_output.ssv,
#           System_State_Files/node_count.ssv,
#           System_State_Files/boredom.ssv
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THE GUI USES THIS FILE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# - On startup, GUI_Skelly.py reads Skelly_Panels.ssv.
# - Each valid line becomes one entry in the "Under The Hood" dropdown.
# - When you select a panel:
#     * The GUI loads the referenced file(s) according to "kind".
#     * It builds x + one or more series[0..N-1].
#     * Then it applies:
#         - Sliding window: only last N points if "Window" spinner > 0
#         - Smoothing: moving average if "Smoothing" spinner > 1
#         - Downsampling: reduces to ~1000 points max so the Pi doesn’t choke
#     * Finally, it draws a simple line graph for that panel.
#
# This means you can add/remove internal plots without changing the GUI code:
# just write the SSV file(s) in one of the supported formats and add a line
# to Skelly_Panels.ssv describing it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADDING YOUR OWN PANELS – QUICK RECAP
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1) Decide which "kind" matches your data:
#       - io_log      : Tick v0 v1 ...
#       - matrix_rows : index v0 v1 ...
#       - xy          : x y
#       - xy_multi    : multiple x y files with shared x
#
# 2) Make sure the engine writes the SSV file(s) in the correct format.
#
# 3) Add a line like:
#
#       my_panel_id ; My Fancy Metric ; xy ; System_State_Files/my_metric.ssv
#
# 4) Restart the GUI. Your new label appears in the "Under The Hood" dropdown.
#
# That’s it. The sliders (window depth, smoothing) and auto-refresh are global
# behaviors applied to whatever panel is currently selected.



# Skelly_Panels.ssv
# panel_id ; label ; kind ; files
#
# Fields:
#   panel_id  - internal name (no spaces)
#   label     - what appears in the dropdown
#   kind      - how to interpret the data: io_log | matrix_rows | xy | xy_multi
#   files     - one or more paths, comma-separated (relative or absolute)

io_aff        ; Afferent I/O Log         ; io_log      ; System_State_Files/afferent_log.ssv
io_eff        ; Efferent I/O Log         ; io_log      ; System_State_Files/efferent_log.ssv

devmap        ; Deviation Map            ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/deviation_mapping.ssv
projection    ; Projection Map           ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/projection.ssv
scores        ; Output Scores            ; matrix_rows ; System_State_Files/output_scores.ssv

trace_metrics ; Trace / Node Metrics     ; xy_multi    ; System_State_Files/trace_valid.ssv,System_State_Files/trace_nearly_valid.ssv,System_State_Files/trace_total_output.ssv,System_State_Files/node_count.ssv,System_State_Files/boredom.ssv
